From e31e2e25789a4f0c232f1a8dfd2aff3b599a348e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:52:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] b43: AC-PHY: femctrl 3 FEM tables (sub 0 and sub 3)

The Netgear R7000, the Linksys EA6900 and the 5 GHz radio of the R6400
have femctrl 3, and set_regtbl_on_femctrl() finds no table for it: sub
3 on the 2.4 GHz radios, sub 0 on the 5 GHz radios.

Sub 0: the R7000 writes the table in both passes of its 5 GHz MMIO
capture (records #6714-#6815, #24771-#24872), and PHY 0x040a = 0x0090
just before (#6704). A read-back on an R6400 v1 gives the same table.

Sub 3: the 2.4 GHz capture has no write of the table, so it is read
back from the R7000 with wl up, at ch 11 and ch 6, all passes identical
(router-data/r7000/fem-table-2g-ch6.txt, new). PHY 0x040a reads 0x0090
there too.

Both take a pre hook: PHY 0x040a bit 8 clear, and PMU regctl 0 bit 2
set, which the R7000 reads back with wl up (0x00200004). The PMU is
outside the capture window, so on an EA6900 with the tables in place:
with bit 2 cleared neither radio sends anything a client can receive
(no beacon in a scan, no client connects, twice); with it set both
work.

Tested on an EA6900 (femctrl 3, sub 3 + sub 0), WRX36 client to a
wired LAN host, 20 s TCP: 2.4 GHz HT20 139 down / 154 up, 5 GHz VHT80
359 down / 382 up Mbit/s, no b43 warnings. This patch is in the build
that passed a 16 h soak (two clients, both bands, 0 b43 errors, 0
replays): 2.4 GHz 143 down / 157 up, 5 GHz 362 / 379 on average.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
